#: src/man/man9/hm2_pci.9.adoc:34 src/man/man9/hostmot2.9.adoc:1
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
msgid "hostmot2(9)"
msgstr "hostmot2"

#. type: Title =
#: src/man/man9/hm2_7i90.9.adoc:1
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
msgid "hm2_7i90(9)"
msgstr "hm2_7i43"

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_7i90.9.adoc:7
msgid ""
"hm2_7i90 - LinuxCNC HAL driver for the Mesa Electronics 7i90 EPP Anything IO "
"board with HostMot2 firmware."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_7i90.9.adoc:11
msgid ""
"**loadrt hm2_7i90** [**ioaddr=**__N__[,_N_...]] "
"[**ioaddr_hi=**__N__[,_N_...]] [**epp_wide=**__N__[,_N_...]] "
"[**debug_epp=**__N__[,_N_...]]"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block _
#: src/man/man9/hm2_7i90.9.adoc:19
msgid ""
"The number of ioaddr indexes/addresses given is used by the driver to "
"determine how many boards to search for. Previously the number of config "
"strings was used, but a blank config string is perfectly acceptable for 7i90."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_7i90.9.adoc:37
msgid ""
"hm2_7i90 is a device driver that interfaces the Mesa 7i90 board with the "
"HostMot2 firmware to the LinuxCNC HAL."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_7i90.9.adoc:41
msgid ""
"The 7i90 firmware is stored on the 7i90 itself, it is not programmed by the "
"driver at load time. The 7i90 firmware can be changed using the mesaflash "
"program."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_7i90.9.adoc:43
msgid "The driver talks with the 7i90 over the parallel port, via EPP."
msgstr "El controlador habla con el 7i90 por el puerto paralelo, con EPP."

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_7i90.9.adoc:49
msgid ""
"The 7i90 communicates with the LinuxCNC computer over EPP, the Enhanced "
"Parallel Port. This provides about 1 MBps of throughput, and the "
"communication latency is very predictable and reasonably low."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_7i90.9.adoc:57
msgid ""
"Note that the popular \"NetMOS\" aka \"MosChip 9805\" PCI parport cards *do* "
"not work. They do not meet the EPP spec, and cannot be reliably used with "
"the 7i90. You have to find another card, sorry."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_7i90.9.adoc:65
msgid ""
"EPP is very reliable under normal circumstances, but bad cabling or "
"excessively long cabling runs may cause communication timeouts. The driver "
"exports a parameter named hm2_7i90.<BoardNum>.io_error to inform HAL of this "
"condition. When the driver detects an EPP timeout, it sets io_error to True "
"and stops communicating with the 7i90 board. Setting io_error back to False "
"makes the driver start trying to communicate with the 7i90 again."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title =
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:1
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
msgid "hm2_eth(9)"
msgstr "hm2_pci"

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:7
msgid ""
"hm2_eth - LinuxCNC HAL driver for the Mesa Electronics Ethernet Anything IO "
"boards, with HostMot2 firmware."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:11
msgid ""
"*loadrt hm2_eth* [**config=**\"__str__[,__str__...]\"] "
"[**board_ip=**__ip__[,__ip__...] ] [**board_mac=**__mac__[,__mac__...] ]"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:15
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
msgid "*board_ip* [default: \"\"]"
msgstr "Área de visualización de texto"

#. type: delimited block _
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:18
msgid ""
"The IP address of the board(s), separated by commas.  As shipped, the board "
"address is 192.168.1.121."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:28
msgid ""
"hm2_eth is a device driver that interfaces Mesa's ethernet based Anything I/"
"O boards (with the HostMot2 firmware) to the LinuxCNC HAL.  The supported "
"boards are: 7I76E, 7I80DB, 7I80HD, 7I92, 7I93, 7I94, 7I95, 7I96, 7I96S, "
"7I97, 7I98. It also supports boards with the litehm2 firmware (https://"
"github.com/sensille/litehm2). The board must have its firmware loaded on the "
"board by the mesaflash(1) program."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:30
msgid ""
"hm2_eth is only available when LinuxCNC is configured with \"uspace\" "
"realtime."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:31 src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:86
#: src/man/man9/hm2_spi.9.adoc:35
#, no-wrap
msgid "INTERFACE CONFIGURATION"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:36
msgid ""
"hm2_eth should be used on a dedicated network interface, with only a cable "
"between the PC and the board. Wireless and USB network interfaces are not "
"suitable."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:43
msgid ""
"These instructions assume your dedicated network interface is \"eth1\", "
"192.168.1/24 is an unused private network, that the hostmot2 board is using "
"the default address of 192.168.1.121, that you are using Debian 7 or "
"similar, and that you do not otherwise use iptables. If any of these are "
"false, you will need to modify the instructions accordingly. After following "
"all the instructions, reboot so that the changes take effect."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:52
msgid ""
"It is particularly important to check that the network 192.168.1/24 is not "
"already the private network used by your internet router, because this is a "
"commonly-used value. If you use another network, you will also need to "
"reconfigure the hostmot2 card to use an IP address on that network by using "
"the mesaflash(1) utility and change jumper settings.  Typically, you will "
"choose one of the networks in the https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"IPv4#Private_networks[Private IPv4 address space.] One common alternative is "
"PC address 10.10.10.1, hostmot2 address 10.10.10.10."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:56
msgid ""
"Use of the dedicated ethernet interface while LinuxCNC is running can cause "
"violation of realtime guarantees. hm2_eth will automatically mitigate most "
"accidental causes of interference."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:57
#, no-wrap
msgid "Configure network with static address"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:61
msgid ""
"Add these lines to the file /etc/network/interfaces to configure the "
"ethernet interface eth1 with a static address:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:67
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"auto eth1\n"
"iface eth1 inet static\n"
"    address 192.168.1.1\n"
"    hardware-irq-coalesce-rx-usecs 0\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:69
#, no-wrap
msgid "PACKET LOSS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:79
msgid ""
"While ethernet is fairly resistant to electrical noise, many systems will "
"not have 100% perfect packet reception. The hm2_eth driver has a limited "
"ability to deal with lost packets. Packet loss is detected by transmitting "
"an expected read or write packet count with each request, and checking the "
"value with each read response. When a lost packet is detected, the `packet-"
"error` pin is asserted in that cycle, the `packet-error-level` pin is "
"increased, and if it reaches a threshold then a permanent low-level I/O "
"error is signaled."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:85
msgid ""
"However, not all hm2 special functions know how to properly recover from "
"lost packets. For instance, the encoder special function does not properly "
"manage the index feature when packets are lost. The author believes that "
"this can lead to rare failures in home-to-index, which can have severe "
"consequences."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:94
msgid ""
"On the other hand, pid-stepper systems will run properly for extended "
"periods of time with packet loss on the order of .01%, as long as following "
"error is increased enough that having stale position feedback does not "
"trigger a following error. Altering the HAL configuration so that during "
"transient packet loss the pid and motion feedback value is equal to the "
"command value, instead of the stale feedback value, appears to improve "
"tuning. This can be accomplished with a *mux2(9)* component for each "
"feedback signal, using *packet-error* as the mux2 *sel* input."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:99
#, fuzzy
msgid ""
"In addition to the pins documented in *hostmot2(9)*, *hm2_eth(9)* creates "
"the following additional pins:"
msgstr "Además de los pines de salida, hay tres pines de control:"

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:100
#, no-wrap
msgid "hm2___<BoardType>__.__<BoardNum>__.packet-error (bit, out)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:102
msgid ""
"This pin is TRUE when the most recent cycle detected a read or write error, "
"and FALSE at other times."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:102
#, no-wrap
msgid "hm2___<BoardType>__.__<BoardNum>__.packet-error-level (s32, out)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:105
msgid ""
"This pin shows the current error level, with higher numbers indicating a "
"greater number of recent detected errors.  The error level is always in the "
"range from 0 to packet-error-limit, inclusive."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:105
#, no-wrap
msgid "hm2___<BoardType>__.__<BoardNum>__.packet-error-exceeded (bit, out)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:107
msgid ""
"This pin is TRUE when the current error level is equal to the maximum, and "
"FALSE at other times."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:112
msgid ""
"In addition to the parameters documented in *hostmot2(9)*, *hm2_eth(9)* "
"creates the following additional parameters:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:113
#, no-wrap
msgid "hm2___<BoardType>__.__<BoardNum>__.packet-error-decrement (s32, rw)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:116
msgid ""
"The amount deducted from `packet-error-level` in a cycle without detected "
"read or write errors, without going below zero."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:116
#, no-wrap
msgid "hm2___<BoardType>__.__<BoardNum>__.packet-error-increment (s32, rw)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:119
msgid ""
"The amount added to `packet-error-level` in a cycle without detected read or "
"write errors, without going above `packet-error-limit`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:119
#, no-wrap
msgid "hm2___<BoardType>__.__<BoardNum>__.packet-error-limit (s32, rw)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:123
msgid ""
"The level at which a detected read or write error is treated as a permanent "
"error. When this error level is reached, the board's `io-error` pin becomes "
"TRUE and the condition must be manually reset."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:123
#, no-wrap
msgid "hm2___<BoardType>__.__<BoardNum>__.packet-read-timeout (s32, rw)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:129
msgid ""
"The length of time that must pass before a read request times out.  If the "
"value is less than or equal to 0, it is interpreted as 80% of the thread "
"period.  If the value is less than 100, it is interpreted as a percentage of "
"the thread period.  Otherwise, it is interpreted as a time in nanoseconds.  "
"In any case, the timeout is never less than 100 microseconds."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:132
msgid ""
"Setting this value too low can cause spurious read errors.  Setting it too "
"high can cause realtime delay errors."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:144
msgid ""
"hm2_eth uses an iptables chain called \"hm2-eth-rules-output\".  That "
"technology is common to control network access to (INPUT chain), through "
"(FORWARD chain) or from (OUTPUT chain) your computer.  Someone who has "
"configured a firewall on Linux has encountered iptables is familiar with "
"that technology.  This chain contains additional rules to control network "
"interface while HAL is running.  The chain is created if it does not exist, "
"and a jump to it is inserted at the beginning of the OUTPUT chain if it is "
"not there already.  If you have an existing iptables setup, you can insert a "
"direct jump from OUTPUT to hm2-eth-rules-output in an order appropriate to "
"your local network."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:148
msgid ""
"At (normal) exit, hm2_eth will remove the rules. After a crash, you can "
"manually clear the rules with *sudo iptables -F hm2-eth-rules-output*; the "
"rules are also removed by a reboot."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:154
msgid ""
"\"hardware-irq-coalesce-rx-usecs\" decreases time waiting to receive a "
"packet on most systems, but on at least some Marvel-chipset NICs it is "
"harmful.  If the line does not improve system performance, then remove it.  "
"A reboot is required for the value to be set back to its power-on default.  "
"This requires the ethtool package to be installed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:159
msgid ""
"Some hostmot2 functions such uart are coded in a way that causes additional "
"latency when used with hm2_eth."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:164
msgid ""
"On the 7i92, the HAL pins for the LEDs are called CR01..CR04, but the "
"silkscreens are CR3..CR6. Depending on the FPGA firmware, the LEDs may "
"initially be under control of the ethernet engine. This can be changed until "
"power cycle with"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:167
#, no-wrap
msgid "elbpcom 01D914000000\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:172
msgid ""
"Depending on firmware version, this driver may cause the hardware error LED "
"to light even though the driver and hardware are functioning normally. This "
"will reportedly be fixed in future bitfile updates from Mesa."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_eth.9.adoc:176
#, fuzzy
msgid "hostmot2(9), elbpcom(1)"
msgstr "hostmot2"

#. type: Title =
#: src/man/man9/hm2_pci.9.adoc:1
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
msgid "hm2_pci(9)"
msgstr "hm2_pci"

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_pci.9.adoc:7
msgid ""
"hm2_pci - LinuxCNC HAL driver for the Mesa Electronics PCI-based Anything IO "
"boards, with HostMot2 firmware."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_pci.9.adoc:11
msgid "*loadrt hm2_pci* [**config=**\"__str__[,__str__...]\"]"
msgstr "*loadrt hm2_pci* [**config=**\"__cadena__[,__cadena__...]\"]"

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_pci.9.adoc:21
msgid ""
"hm2_pci is a device driver that interfaces Mesa's PCI and PC-104/Plus based "
"Anything I/O boards (with the HostMot2 firmware) to the LinuxCNC HAL."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_pci.9.adoc:25
msgid ""
"The supported boards are: the 5i20, 5i21, 5i22, 5i23, 5i24, and 5i25 (all on "
"PCI); the 4i65, 4i68, and 4i69 (on PC-104/Plus), and the 3x20 (using a 6i68 "
"or 7i68 carrier card) and 6i25 (on PCI Express)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_pci.9.adoc:30
msgid ""
"The driver optionally programs the board's FPGA with firmware when it "
"registers the board with the hostmot2 driver. The firmware to load is "
"specified in the *config* modparam, as described in the hostmot2(9)  "
"manpage, in the _config modparam_ section."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title =
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:1
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
msgid "hm2_rpspi(9)"
msgstr "hm2_pci"

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:8
#, fuzzy
msgid ""
"hm2_rpspi - This driver has been superseded by the hm2_spix driver. LinuxCNC "
"HAL driver for the Mesa Electronics SPI Anything IO boards, with HostMot2 "
"firmware."
msgstr ""
"(((hm2_7i43)))Controlador HAL para tarjetas EPP Anything Mesa Electronics "
"7i43 EPP con HostMot2."

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:13
msgid "*loadrt hm2_rpspi*"
msgstr "*loadrt hm2_rpspi*"

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:16
msgid "HostMot2 config strings, described in the *hostmot2*(9) manpage."
msgstr ""
"Cadenas de configuración HostMot2, descritas en la página del manual de "
"*hostmot2*(9)."

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:16
#, no-wrap
msgid "*spiclk_rate* [default: 31250]"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:18
msgid "Specify the SPI clock rate in kHz. See *SPI CLOCK RATES* below."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:18
#, no-wrap
msgid "*spiclk_rate_rd* [default: -1 (same as *spiclk_rate*)]"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:22
msgid ""
"Specify the SPI read clock rate in kHz. Usually you read and write at the "
"same speed. However, you may want to reduce the reading speed if the round-"
"trip is too long (see *SPI CLOCK RATES* below)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:22
#, no-wrap
msgid "*spiclk_base* [default: 400000000]"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:32
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This is the SPI clock divider calculation fallback value. Usually, the base rate is read from _/sys/kernel/debug/clk/vpu/clk_rate_ and used in the divider calculation (for the Rpi3 it should be 250 MHz). The *spiclk_base* is _only_ used as a fallback if the system's cannot be read. It is normally safe (and recommended) that you leave this parameter as is.\n"
"You should set this manually to 250000000 if your system does not provide access to the kernel clock settings. Otherwise, your SPI clock frequency will be only 62.5% of the requested value."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:32
#, no-wrap
msgid "*spi_pull_miso* [default: 1 (pull-down)]"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:33
#, no-wrap
msgid "*spi_pull_mosi* [default: 1 (pull-down)]"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:34
#, no-wrap
msgid "*spi_pull_sclk* [default: 1 (pull-down)]"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:38
msgid ""
"Enable or disable pull-up/pull-down on the SPI lines. A value of 0 disables "
"any pull-up/down on the pin. A value of 1 means pull-down and 2 means pull-"
"up. The chip enable line(s) are always pull-up enabled."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:38
#, no-wrap
msgid "*spi_probe* [default: 1]"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:41
msgid ""
"Probe SPI port and CE lines for a card. This is a bit-field indicating which "
"combinations of SPI and CE should be probed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:42
msgid "1 = SPI0/CE0,"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:43
msgid "2 = SPI0/CE1,"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:44
msgid "4 = SPI1/CE0,"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:45
msgid "8 = SPI1/CE1,"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:46
msgid "16 = SPI1/CE2."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:49
msgid ""
"The probe is performed exactly in above order. Any boards found will be "
"numbered 0...4 in the order found. See also *INTERFACE CONFIGURATION* below."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:53
msgid ""
"It is an error if a probe fails and the driver will abort. The SPI0/SPI1 "
"peripherals are located at GPIO pins (with 40-pin I/O header pin-number in "
"parentheses):"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:54
msgid "SPI0: MOSI=10(19), MISO=9(21), SCLK=11(23), CE0=8(24), CE1=7(26)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:55
msgid ""
"SPI1: MOSI=20(38), MISO=19(35), SCLK=21(40), CE0=18(12), CE1=17(11), "
"CE2=16(36)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:56
#, no-wrap
msgid "*spi_debug* [default: -1]"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:61
msgid ""
"Set the message level of the running process.  The message level is set if "
"*spi_debug* is set to a positive value between 0 and 5, where 0 means no "
"messages at all and 5 means everything.  A value of -1 does not touch the "
"current message level."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:65
msgid ""
"Caveat Emptor: Changing the message level is process-wide and all modules "
"within the process will spit out messages at the requested level.  This may "
"cause quite some clutter in your terminal."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:72
msgid ""
"hm2_rpspi is a device driver for the Raspberry Pi 2/3 that interfaces Mesa's "
"SPI based Anything I/O boards (with the HostMot2 firmware) to the LinuxCNC "
"HAL. This driver is not based on the linux spidev driver, but on a dedicated "
"BCM2835-SPI driver."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:77
msgid ""
"It is *strongly* recommended that you unload/disable the kernel's spidev "
"driver by disabling it using *raspi-config*. Please note that having both "
"kernel and user-space SPI drivers installed can result in unexpected "
"interactions and system instabilities."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:79 src/man/man9/hm2_spi.9.adoc:29
msgid "The supported boards are: 7I90HD."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:82
msgid ""
"The board must have a compatible firmware (ie.: 7i90_spi_svst4_8.bit)  "
"loaded on the board by the *mesaflash*(1) program."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:85
msgid ""
"hm2_rpspi is only available when LinuxCNC is configured with \"uspace\" "
"realtime. It works with Raspian and PREEMPT_RT kernel."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/man/man9/hm2_rpspi.9.adoc:93
msgid ""
"Up to five devices (7i90 boards) are supported. Two on SPI0 and three on "
"SPI1. It is recommended that you, at most, use two devices and each device "
"connected to a separate SPI port. You can choose which CE lines you prefer "
"or fit the design and setup the *spi_probe* parameter to instruct the driver "
"where to search for the board(s)."
msgstr ""

